#Phishing - Complete Notes
1. Email Basics
Email was popularized in the 1970s by Ray Tomlinson, who introduced the @ symbol.
________________________________________
2. Email Address Structure
An email address contains:
Username
•	Identifies the recipient's mailbox.
@ Symbol
•	Separates username from domain.
Domain
•	Identifies the mail server.
Example:
david@tryhackme.com
•	Username → david
•	Domain → tryhackme.com
________________________________________
3. Email Protocols
SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol)
•	Used to send emails.
POP3 (Post Office Protocol)
•	Downloads emails to one device.
•	Emails may be removed from the server.
IMAP (Internet Message Access Protocol)
•	Syncs emails across multiple devices.
•	Emails remain stored on the server.
________________________________________
4. Email Journey
1.	User sends an email.
2.	SMTP sends it to the sender's mail server.
3.	DNS finds the recipient's mail server.
4.	Email travels across the Internet.
5.	Recipient retrieves email using POP3 or IMAP.
________________________________________
5. Email Structure
An email has two main parts:
Email Header
Contains metadata:
•	From
•	To
•	Reply-To
•	Subject
•	Date
•	Sender IP
•	Delivery information
Email Body
Contains the message:
•	Plain text
•	HTML
________________________________________
6. Message Source Analysis
Viewing message source reveals:
•	Full headers
•	Raw email body
•	Sender IP
•	Technical information
Shortcut:
Ctrl + U
Useful for phishing investigations.
________________________________________
7. HTML Emails
HTML emails may contain:
•	Images
•	Links
•	Formatting
•	Embedded content
Analyzing HTML source helps detect:
•	Hidden links
•	Suspicious elements
•	Malicious content
________________________________________
8. Email Attachments
Important attachment headers:
Content-Type
•	Defines file type.
•	Example: application/pdf
Content-Disposition
•	Shows attachment name and file type.
Content-Transfer-Encoding
•	Shows encoding method.
•	Commonly Base64.
Base64 data can be decoded to recover the original file.
Tools:
•	CyberChef
•	Base64 decoder
________________________________________
9. Malicious Email Types
Spam
•	Unwanted bulk emails sent to many recipients.
•	Malicious spam is called malspam.
Phishing
•	Emails impersonating trusted entities.
•	Goal: steal sensitive information.
Spear Phishing
•	Targeted phishing against a specific person or organization.
•	Uses personal information.
Whaling
•	Spear phishing targeting high-level executives:
o	CEO
o	CFO
o	Managers
Goal:
•	Steal sensitive data.
•	Gain financial access.
Smishing
•	Phishing through SMS/text messages.
•	Targets mobile users.
Vishing
•	Phishing through voice calls.
•	Uses social engineering over the phone.
________________________________________
10. Phishing Email Indicators
Common signs:
Spoofed From Address
•	Fake sender pretending to be trusted.
Example:
noreply@microsof.com
Urgency
Creates pressure:
"Your account will be locked in 24 hours"
Brand Impersonation
Copies:
•	Logos
•	Colors
•	Company design
Grammar Issues
•	Strange wording.
•	Spelling mistakes.
Generic Content
Example:
"Dear Customer"
instead of using a real name.
Hidden or Shortened Links
•	Disguise the real destination.
•	Example:
o	bit.ly links
Malicious Attachments
Examples:
•	Fake invoices
•	Malware files
Example:
invoice.pdf.exe
________________________________________
11. Safe Email Analysis
Never click suspicious:
•	Links
•	Attachments
•	IP addresses
Use defanging.
Example:
Original:
http://www.suspiciousdomain.com
Defanged:
hxxp[://]www[.]suspiciousdomain[.]com
Common changes:
. → [.]
:// → [://]
Purpose:
•	Prevent accidental clicks.
•	Safely share indicators.
________________________________________
12. Phishing Example: Fake PayPal Receipt
Attackers often impersonate trusted companies to trick users.
Example:
•	Fake PayPal transaction receipt.
________________________________________
Techniques Used
Spoofed Email Address
•	Fake sender address appears trusted.
•	Used to gain credibility.
Example:
Displayed:
service@paypal.com
Actual:
gibberish@sultanbogor.com
The real sender does not belong to PayPal.
________________________________________
URL Shortening
Attackers use URL shorteners to:
•	Hide the final destination.
•	Make malicious links harder to detect.
Security rule:
Never click links before checking where they lead.
________________________________________
Branded HTML
Attackers copy:
•	Logos
•	Colors
•	Company layouts
Goal:
•	Make fake emails look authentic.
________________________________________
Email Investigation
Subject Analysis
Phishing subjects often:
•	Create urgency.
•	Trigger emotional reactions.
Example:
"Your payment was successful"
or
"Your account will be closed"
________________________________________
Header Analysis
Check:
•	From address
•	To address
•	Subject
•	Sender domain
Red flags:
•	Sender does not match claimed company.
•	Strange recipient address.
•	Suspicious domains.
________________________________________
Body Analysis
Fake PayPal email example:
•	Looks like a purchase receipt.
•	Contains gift card transaction details.
•	No attachments.
•	Contains a suspicious button:
"Cancel the order"
________________________________________
Button Investigation
Inspecting raw HTML reveals:
•	Hidden links.
•	Redirect URLs.
•	Actual destination.
Attackers hide malicious pages behind shortened URLs.
