13. Phishing Example: Fake Shipping Notification
This phishing email imitates a shipping notification to create urgency and trick users into clicking a malicious link.
Attackers use fake delivery messages because people are more likely to react quickly when they think they have an important package.
________________________________________
Phishing Techniques Used
Spoofed Email Address
•	The attacker pretends to be a trusted delivery service.
•	Goal:
o	Gain credibility.
o	Make the victim trust the email.
Example:
Displayed name:
Distribution Center
Actual email:
contact@beginpro.club
Red flag:
•	Display name does not match the real sender address.
________________________________________
Pixel Tracking
Attackers embed invisible images inside emails.
Purpose:
•	Detect when the email is opened.
•	Send information back to the attacker's server.
Tracking pixels are often:
•	Very small images.
•	Hidden inside the email HTML.
Because of this, many email providers block images automatically.
________________________________________
Link Manipulation
Attackers hide malicious destinations behind:
•	Fake tracking numbers.
•	Disguised hyperlinks.
•	Fake delivery buttons.
Goal:
•	Make users click a malicious website.
________________________________________
First Observations
Subject Line
The subject creates urgency:
Example:
•	Fake tracking number.
•	Package delivery notification.
Goal:
•	Encourage the user to check the link quickly.
________________________________________
From Address
Immediate warning sign:
Displayed:
Distribution Center
Actual:
contact@beginpro.club
The sender does not match a legitimate shipping company.
________________________________________
Hyperlink
The email contains a tracking link.
Problem:
•	The link looks related to shipping.
•	The real destination is unknown.
Always inspect links before clicking.
________________________________________
Image Blocking
Email providers like Yahoo may block images automatically.
Reason:
Images can contain tracking pixels.
When loaded, they can tell attackers:
•	When the email was opened.
•	Information about the recipient.
________________________________________
Raw Source Analysis
Checking the email source reveals:
•	Image source URLs.
•	Hidden tracking pixels.
•	Redirect links.
•	Actual hyperlink destinations.
The raw HTML can expose malicious elements hidden in the normal email view.
14. Phishing Example: Multi-Stage Redirection & Credential Harvesting Campaign
This phishing campaign uses a multi-stage redirection chain to steal user credentials.
Attackers use trusted services and brands such as:
•	Microsoft 
•	OneDrive 
•	Adobe 
Goal:
•	Create trust. 
•	Hide the final malicious destination. 
•	Trick users into entering login credentials. 
________________________________________
Phishing Techniques Used
Artificial Urgency
Attackers create pressure by introducing a limited time window.
Example:
•	Document download link expires the same day. 
Purpose:
•	Force the victim to act quickly. 
•	Prevent careful analysis of the email. 
________________________________________
Brand Impersonation
Attackers imitate trusted companies.
Examples:
•	Microsoft 
•	OneDrive 
•	Adobe 
Purpose:
•	Create a false sense of security. 
•	Make the phishing page appear legitimate. 
________________________________________
Link Redirection
Attackers use multiple URLs in a chain.
Example flow:
Email link
↓
Fake OneDrive page
↓
Fake Adobe page
↓
Fake login portal
Purpose:
•	Hide the real malicious destination. 
•	Avoid detection by email security filters. 
________________________________________
Credential Harvesting
Attackers create fake login pages.
Goal:
•	Capture usernames. 
•	Capture passwords. 
•	Send stolen credentials to attacker-controlled servers. 
________________________________________
First Observations
Send Date
The email was sent on:
Thursday, July 15th, 2021
________________________________________
Expiration Date
The email creates urgency by claiming:
•	The document download link expires soon. 
Purpose:
•	Encourage immediate action. 
•	Reduce the chance that the victim investigates the email. 
________________________________________
Download Document Here Button
The email contains an action button:
Download Document Here
This button starts the phishing chain.
________________________________________
Redirection Chain Analysis
After clicking the download button:
Stage 1 - Fake OneDrive Page
The victim is redirected to a page pretending to be a legitimate OneDrive document share.
Attackers use:
•	Microsoft branding. 
•	Familiar document-sharing layouts. 
Goal:
•	Make the victim believe the document is safe. 
________________________________________
Stage 2 - Fake Adobe Page
Interacting with the fake OneDrive page redirects the victim again.
The next page impersonates Adobe.
Warning signs:
•	Suspicious URL. 
•	Strange instructions. 
•	Incorrect page behavior. 
________________________________________
Stage 3 - Credential Harvesting Portal
The final page asks the victim to sign in using their email provider.
Example:
•	Outlook login page. 
Purpose:
•	Steal authentication credentials. 
________________________________________
Fake Login Page Analysis
The victim attempts to log in using Outlook.
Important:
Even if valid credentials are entered:
•	Login does not actually happen. 
•	The credentials are sent directly to the attacker. 
•	The page displays a fake error message. 
Example:
"Invalid Credentials"
________________________________________
Why Attackers Use Fake Login Pages
A fake login page allows attackers to:
•	Collect usernames. 
•	Collect passwords. 
•	Access corporate accounts. 
•	Perform further attacks. 
Possible next steps:
•	Account takeover. 
•	Internal reconnaissance. 
•	Privilege escalation. 
•	Data theft. 
________________________________________
AI and Modern Phishing
Older phishing emails often contained:
•	Grammar mistakes. 
•	Spelling errors. 
•	Poor formatting. 
Today:
Attackers use AI to create:
•	Professional-looking emails. 
•	Correct grammar. 
•	Realistic branding. 
•	Convincing messages. 
Important:
Grammar mistakes are no longer a reliable indicator of phishing.
Always analyze:
•	Sender address. 
•	Links. 
•	Redirects. 
•	Email headers. 
•	Website authenticity. 
________________________________________
Investigation Checklist
When analyzing similar phishing emails check:
Email Header
Look for:
•	Fake sender addresses. 
•	Suspicious domains. 
•	Mismatched display names. 
________________________________________
Links
Check:
•	Final destination. 
•	Redirect chains. 
•	Suspicious domains. 
Never trust:
•	Buttons. 
•	Shortened URLs. 
•	Embedded links. 
________________________________________
Login Pages
Verify:
•	Correct domain. 
•	HTTPS certificate. 
•	Official company URL. 
Never enter credentials into:
•	Unknown pages. 
•	Suspicious redirects. 
•	Fake document portals. 
15. Phishing Example: Fake Netflix Billing Notification
This phishing email impersonates a trusted streaming service (Netflix) and uses a fake billing notification to pressure the victim into taking action.
Unlike previous phishing examples that mainly used malicious links inside emails, this attack uses a malicious attachment to hide the real phishing destination.
Attackers use fake billing problems because users are more likely to react quickly when they believe their account has been suspended.
________________________________________
Phishing Techniques Used
Spoofed Email Address
Attackers manipulate the sender information to appear legitimate.
Example:
Displayed name:
Netflix billing
Red flags:
•	Display name does not match the real sender address. 
•	Sender domain is not associated with Netflix. 
Purpose:
•	Gain victim trust. 
•	Make the email appear official. 
________________________________________
Sense of Urgency
The email claims that:
•	The user's account has been suspended. 
•	Immediate action is required. 
Purpose:
•	Create panic. 
•	Force the victim to act without investigating. 
Example:
"Your account has been suspended. Update your payment information immediately."
________________________________________
Brand Impersonation
Attackers copy legitimate company designs.
They use:
•	Netflix logos. 
•	HTML email templates. 
•	Company colors. 
•	Similar formatting. 
Goal:
•	Make the phishing email look authentic. 
•	Trick users into believing it is a real Netflix message. 
________________________________________
Poor Grammar and Typos
The email contains:
•	Spelling mistakes. 
•	Incorrect wording. 
•	Unnatural phrases. 
Example:
Incorrect variations of:
Netflix
can be used as a warning sign.
Important:
Grammar mistakes alone are no longer enough to detect phishing because AI can generate professional-looking emails.
________________________________________
Malicious Attachments
Instead of placing a direct malicious link inside the email, attackers attach a file.
Example:
PDF attachment.
Purpose:
•	Bypass some email filters. 
•	Hide the malicious URL. 
•	Make the attack appear more legitimate. 
________________________________________
First Observations
Email Subject
The subject creates urgency.
Example:
•	Receiver's ID was suspended. 
•	Immediate action required. 
Goal:
•	Encourage quick reaction. 
•	Make the victim open the attachment. 
________________________________________
From Address
Warning signs:
•	Display name claims to be Netflix. 
•	Actual sender does not belong to Netflix. 
•	Domain mismatch. 
Always compare:
Display name
with
Actual email address.
________________________________________
Brand Impersonation
The email uses:
•	Rendered HTML. 
•	Netflix branding. 
•	Similar visual design. 
Purpose:
•	Create trust. 
•	Make the victim believe the email is official. 
________________________________________
Email Body Analysis
The email claims:
•	There is a problem with billing information. 
•	The user must update payment details. 
•	The attached PDF contains instructions. 
The victim is instructed to:
1.	Open the PDF attachment. 
2.	Click the payment update link. 
3.	Enter information. 
________________________________________
Attachment Analysis
The attached PDF contains:
A button/link:
Update Payment Account
The link redirects to:
•	A URL not associated with Netflix. 
•	A potentially malicious website. 
Important:
A PDF attachment does not automatically mean the file is safe.
Documents can contain:
•	Embedded links. 
•	Malicious content. 
•	Redirects. 
________________________________________
Additional Red Flags
Suspicious Phone Number Format
The email contains:
•	An unusual phone number format. 
This may indicate:
•	Fake contact information. 
•	Attempt to appear legitimate. 
________________________________________
Fake Trust Building
Attackers may include:
A legitimate Netflix Help Center domain.
Purpose:
•	Make the email look more believable. 
•	Distract from malicious elements. 
Important:
Attackers often combine real information with fake content to increase credibility.
________________________________________
Investigation Process
When analyzing similar phishing emails:
Check Sender
Look for:
•	Domain mismatch. 
•	Fake display names. 
•	Suspicious addresses. 
________________________________________
Analyze Attachments
Check:
•	File type. 
•	Embedded links. 
•	Redirect destinations. 
Do not open attachments unless they are verified.
________________________________________
Inspect HTML Source
Look for:
•	Hidden URLs. 
•	External resources. 
•	Suspicious code. 
16. Phishing Example: Fake Apple Support Billing Notification
This phishing email impersonates Apple Support and attempts to trick the victim into opening a malicious attachment.
Unlike previous phishing examples that used email body content or visible links to convince the user, this attack uses:
•	A completely blank email body. 
•	A suspicious attachment. 
•	A fake billing notification. 
The attacker relies on urgency and curiosity about an unauthorized purchase to make the victim interact with the file.
________________________________________
Phishing Techniques Used
Spoofed Email Address
Attackers manipulate the sender information to appear as:
Apple Support
Red flags:
•	Display name does not match the actual sender. 
•	Sender domain is not associated with Apple. 
•	Typos appear in the sender address. 
Purpose:
•	Create trust. 
•	Make the email appear to come from a legitimate company. 
________________________________________
BCC Recipient Hiding
The victim receives the email through:
Blind Carbon Copy (BCC)
Meaning:
•	The recipient is hidden from other recipients. 
•	The email was not directly addressed to the victim. 
Why attackers use BCC:
•	Hide the real recipient list. 
•	Send the same phishing email to many targets. 
•	Make the email appear more legitimate. 
Red flag:
A billing notification normally should be addressed directly to the account owner.
________________________________________
Sense of Urgency
The email claims:
•	There was an unauthorized purchase. 
•	Immediate action is required. 
Example:
"Action Required"
Purpose:
•	Create panic. 
•	Encourage the victim to open the attachment quickly. 
•	Prevent careful investigation. 
________________________________________
Poor Grammar and Typos
The email contains:
•	Spelling mistakes. 
•	Incorrect formatting. 
•	Errors in email addresses. 
These mistakes can indicate:
•	Fake sender. 
•	Automated phishing campaign. 
Important:
Modern attackers may use AI to remove obvious grammar mistakes, so other indicators should also be checked.
________________________________________
Malicious Attachment
The email contains:
A .dot file.
.dot file:
•	Microsoft Word Template file. 
•	Can contain embedded content. 
•	Unusual format for a billing receipt. 
Why attackers use attachments:
•	Avoid simple link detection. 
•	Hide malicious redirects. 
•	Encourage the victim to open a document. 
________________________________________
First Observations
Email Subject
The subject creates urgency.
Example:
•	Unauthorized purchase notification. 
•	Action required. 
Goal:
•	Make the victim believe their account is compromised. 
•	Encourage immediate action. 
________________________________________
From Address
Warning signs:
•	Display name: 
Apple Support
does not match:
•	Actual sender address. 
•	Sender domain. 
Additional problems:
•	Typos in the From address. 
•	Typos in the To address. 
Always verify:
•	Sender name. 
•	Sender domain. 
•	Email address spelling. 
________________________________________
Blind Carbon Copy (BCC)
The recipient was:
•	Not directly emailed. 
•	Added through BCC. 
This is suspicious because:
•	Legitimate account notifications normally identify the customer. 
•	Attackers often hide mass phishing campaigns using BCC. 
________________________________________
Attachment Analysis
The email body is:
Completely blank
This is suspicious because:
•	There is no explanation. 
•	The only action available is opening the attachment. 
The attachment:
.dot Microsoft Word Template file
contains:
•	An embedded image. 
•	A hidden redirect link. 
________________________________________
Embedded Link Investigation
When the victim clicks the large image inside the document:
They are redirected to:
•	A phishing website. 
The URL contains familiar words such as:
•	apps 
•	ios 
Attackers use these terms to appear legitimate.
________________________________________
Malicious URL Indicators
Warning signs:
Excessive URL Length
A very long URL may indicate:
•	Redirect chains. 
•	Hidden tracking. 
•	Obfuscated destinations. 
________________________________________
Fake Legitimate Terms
Attackers include words related to trusted brands.
Examples:
•	Apple. 
•	iOS. 
•	Apps. 
Purpose:
•	Make the URL look trustworthy. 
________________________________________
Investigation Process
When analyzing suspicious attachments:
Check File Type
Ask:
•	Is this file type expected? 
•	Does it match the email purpose? 
Example:
A .dot template file is unusual for a purchase receipt.
________________________________________
Analyze Embedded Content
Look for:
•	Images containing links. 
•	Hidden redirects. 
•	External resources. 
________________________________________
Verify Destination URLs
Check:
•	Domain ownership. 
•	URL structure. 
•	Redirect behavior. 
Never trust URLs only because they contain familiar words.
17. Phishing Example: Fake DHL Shipping Notification
This phishing email impersonates a DHL Express shipping notification to trick the victim into opening a malicious Excel attachment.
Unlike previous examples where attackers mainly used fake login pages or document links, this attack uses:
•	Spoofed shipping notifications. 
•	Branded HTML emails. 
•	A malicious Excel file. 
•	An embedded link that attempts to execute malware. 
The attacker uses the reputation of DHL to create trust and convince the victim to open the attachment.
________________________________________
Phishing Techniques Used
Spoofed Email Address
Attackers modify the sender information to appear as:
DHL Express
Red flags:
•	Display name does not match the real sender. 
•	Sender domain is not associated with DHL. 
Purpose:
•	Make the email appear official. 
•	Increase the chance that the victim opens the attachment. 
________________________________________
Brand Impersonation
Attackers recreate DHL branding using:
•	HTML email templates. 
•	Logos. 
•	Company colors. 
•	Similar formatting. 
Goal:
•	Create a realistic-looking notification. 
•	Make the phishing email appear legitimate. 
________________________________________
Malicious Attachments
The email contains:
.xlsx Excel document
Purpose:
•	Deliver malicious content. 
•	Hide the attack inside a seemingly normal business document. 
The attachment contains:
•	A clickable link. 
•	A hidden next stage of the attack. 
________________________________________
First Observations
Email Subject
The subject creates the impression that:
•	DHL is delivering a package. 
•	The recipient needs to check shipping information. 
Goal:
•	Encourage the victim to open the attachment. 
________________________________________
From Address
Warning signs:
Displayed name:
DHL Express
Problems:
•	Sender domain does not belong to DHL. 
•	Display name is only an imitation. 
Always verify:
•	Sender domain. 
•	Email address. 
•	Company ownership. 
________________________________________
Brand Impersonation
The email body uses:
•	DHL-style HTML. 
•	Logos. 
•	Company formatting. 
Purpose:
•	Build trust. 
•	Hide the malicious intent. 
________________________________________
Email Body and Attachment Analysis
The email body contains limited information.
The main part of the attack is inside the attached:
.xlsx file
________________________________________
Geographic Inconsistencies
The document contains multiple suspicious indicators:
Sender Location
•	Uses a German domain. 
Invoice Location
•	Addressed to a city in India. 
Document Language
•	Contains Mandarin text. 
These conflicting details are a warning sign.
Legitimate business documents usually have:
•	Consistent locations. 
•	Consistent language. 
•	Matching company information. 
________________________________________
Excel Attachment Analysis
The Excel file contains:
•	A single clickable hyperlink. 
The link is designed to:
•	Continue the attack. 
•	Download malicious content. 
•	Execute code on the victim's system. 
________________________________________
Executable Payload
When the hyperlink inside the Excel document is clicked:
It attempts to download and execute:
regasms.exe
The system produces an error, preventing execution in the testing environment.
However, the attempt shows the attacker's intention:
•	Execute code on the victim machine. 
•	Gain further access. 
________________________________________
Possible Impact If Successfully Executed
Establish Persistence
The attacker could:
•	Create a backdoor. 
•	Add scheduled tasks. 
•	Maintain access after reboot. 
Goal:
•	Keep control of the infected machine. 
________________________________________
Data Exfiltration
The attacker could steal:
•	Sensitive documents. 
•	Credentials. 
•	Browser-stored passwords. 
•	Personal information. 
Goal:
•	Sell data. 
•	Perform additional attacks. 
________________________________________
Deploy Ransomware
The attacker could:
•	Encrypt files. 
•	Disrupt business operations. 
•	Demand payment for recovery. 
________________________________________
Investigation Process
When analyzing suspicious shipping emails:
Check Sender
Look for:
•	Fake display names. 
•	Incorrect domains. 
•	Mismatched company information. 
________________________________________
Analyze Attachments
Check:
•	File type. 
•	Embedded links. 
•	Suspicious behavior. 
Examples:
•	Excel files containing external links. 
•	Documents requesting actions. 
•	Unexpected attachments. 
________________________________________
Inspect URLs
Look for:
•	Unknown domains. 
