18. Email Analysis Artifacts
When analyzing a suspicious or malicious email, the first objective is to collect artifacts that help determine whether the email is legitimate or part of a phishing attack.
Artifacts are pieces of information extracted from the email that can later be used for:
•	Threat intelligence lookups. 
•	Reputation checks. 
•	IOC (Indicators of Compromise) identification. 
•	Malware analysis. 
•	Incident response. 
Collecting these artifacts is the foundation of every phishing investigation.
________________________________________
Header Artifacts
The email header contains valuable technical information about the message.
When analyzing the header, pay attention to the following artifacts.
________________________________________
Sender Email Address
Question:
Where did the email originate?
Check:
•	Sender username. 
•	Sender domain. 
•	Whether the sender matches the organization it claims to represent. 
Red flags:
•	Misspelled domains. 
•	Free email providers pretending to be companies. 
•	Domain spoofing. 
________________________________________
Sender IP Address
Question:
What is the source IP address?
The sender IP can be used to:
•	Perform reverse IP lookups. 
•	Identify the sending mail server. 
•	Check reputation databases. 
•	Determine the geographical origin of the email. 
A suspicious IP may indicate:
•	Spam infrastructure. 
•	Compromised servers. 
•	Known malicious hosts. 
________________________________________
Email Subject
Question:
Does the subject create urgency or encourage immediate action?
Common phishing subjects include:
•	Account suspended. 
•	Payment failed. 
•	Action required. 
•	Security alert. 
•	Invoice attached. 
Purpose:
•	Pressure the victim into acting quickly. 
________________________________________
Recipient Address
Check:
•	To 
•	CC 
•	BCC 
Questions:
•	Was the email sent directly? 
•	Was it mass distributed? 
•	Is the recipient expected? 
The use of BCC may indicate:
•	Bulk phishing campaigns. 
•	Hidden recipient lists. 
________________________________________
Reply-To Address
Question:
Where will replies actually be sent?
Sometimes attackers use:
•	Legitimate-looking From addresses. 
but configure:
•	A different Reply-To address. 
Purpose:
•	Redirect communication to the attacker. 
Always compare:
•	From 
•	Reply-To 
________________________________________
Date and Time
Check:
•	Date sent. 
•	Time sent. 
•	Time zone. 
Questions:
•	Does the timestamp make sense? 
•	Was the email sent outside normal business hours? 
•	Does it match the claimed organization? 
________________________________________
Email Body Artifacts
The email body also contains valuable indicators that should be analyzed.
________________________________________
URLs and Hyperlinks
Identify:
•	Every embedded link. 
•	Hidden hyperlinks. 
•	Buttons. 
•	Redirects. 
Always:
•	Expand shortened URLs. 
•	Verify the destination domain. 
•	Inspect redirect chains. 
Never trust displayed text alone.
________________________________________
Attachment Names
Check:
•	File names. 
•	File extensions. 
•	Whether the attachment matches the email context. 
Examples of suspicious attachments:
•	invoice.pdf.exe 
•	payment.dot 
•	shipment.xlsx 
•	update.docm 
Unexpected attachment types should always be investigated.
________________________________________
Attachment Hash
